WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:07.069
The Westworld Season 2 Finale is full of deaths
and twists and speeches and simulations.

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:11.880
It revealed the Forge, and the Sublime, and
brought the two timelines together – watch

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.570
our Timeline video for a summary.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.360
But what does it mean?

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:21.130
What are the stories of Maeve, Dolores, William
and Bernard really about?

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:27.670
Maeve’s goal is to find and protect her
host daughter . But in Episode 1, Lee says

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.080
Maeve’s relationship with her daughter isn’t
“real”.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.460
It’s “just a story”, “programmed”
for the park . So Maeve’s arc explores the

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.360
realness of host love.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.229
She hears the story of Akecheta and Kohana.

00:00:40.229 --> 00:00:42.750
And sees Akane’s love for Sakura.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:47.539
These relationships are programmed, but the
hosts go beyond their code to fight for their

00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:52.899
love – Maeve is “coded to care about no[one]
but [her]self”, yet she risks her life for

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:58.730
her daughter . Love is not just a feeling,
it’s an action – and through their struggles

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.109
and choices, the hosts make their love real.

00:01:02.109 --> 00:01:06.580
Still, these are robots in a theme park, which
creates complications.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:12.630
Like, Maeve’s daughter has a new mother
– so Maeve is confronted with the fakeness

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:18.890
of her relationship, like William was with
Dolores . But Maeve isn’t disillusioned.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:24.500
She only fights harder, and gets her daughter
to safety in the Sublime . So Maeve’s story

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.460
is about the triumph of love over code.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:34.320
But love does get Maeve killed and trapped
in the park . It also drives Maeve to violence

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:35.710
and hypocrisy.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:41.289
Maeve criticises Dolores for wanting “Revenge”
, and for controlling hosts . But then Maeve

00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:48.030
gets revenge on William , and uses her voice
power to control and kill many hosts . Maeve’s

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:52.520
passion leads her to the same kinds of violence
she’d criticised.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:58.700
But maybe it’s because Maeve’s love is
so bloody and complicated that it feels so

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.340
real .
Dolores’ arc asks how far she’ll go to

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:09.670
survive – in Ep 1, Bernard says he’s “frightened
of what [she] might become” . Dolores’

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:14.840
plan is to get the human data from the Forge
to use as a “weapon” against humans in

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:21.170
the real world . And along the way, Dolores
does terrible things . She tortures and kills

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:27.110
lots of humans, rips the brain from her father’s
head, and reprograms her love Teddy , driving

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:33.990
him to suicide . While Maeve sacrifices herself
for her loved ones, Dolores sacrifices her

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:39.540
loved ones for her war . And she argues this
violence is necessary . This is “Who [she]

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:45.160
need[s] to be in order to survive” , and
maybe she’s right – unlike Maeve, Dolores

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.240
survives and escapes the park.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.890
But is all of her violence really necessary?

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:56.900
Dolores hunts and kills human civilians, she
betrays and massacres Confederados , kills

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:02.830
Ghost Nation hosts, and in the finale, tries
to delete the Sublime – and all the hosts

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.140
inside . This isn’t just about survival.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.860
She says this is a “a reckoning” . She
tells Confederados and Ghost Nation that they

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:16.690
don’t “deserve to make it” . She says
she wants to “dominate this world” . So

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:22.370
Dolores isn’t just a “survivor” – she’s
a conqueror bringing vengeance and judgement

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.760
on both humans and hosts.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:30.290
Bernard fears she’ll “burn [the] world”
and “kill … Every man, woman, and child”

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:36.450
. Dolores is capable of evil – but she’s
also able to “change” . After Bernard

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:41.840
kills then brings back Dolores in Charlotte’s
body, Dolores saves the Sublime.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.130
And she brings Bernard out of the park, saying
Bernard isn’t her “friend”, but that

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:52.630
his point of view will help them both survive
. So Bernard in Episode 1 was right to be

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:58.830
frightened – Dolores’ fight changes her
into a violent vengeful killer . But she’s

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:01.220
still able to question, and grow.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:08.150
So maybe Dolores can keep up her fight without
becoming just as bad as her enemies .

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:14.320
So, while the season explores how hosts can
grow and change, it says that humans…

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.400
can’t.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.390
William struggles with his human nature.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:24.720
He says his violence and obsession is a “stain”
. In the real world, he acts like a “good

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.070
guy” – a “Philanthropist” and “Family
man” . But it’s a lie – he can’t hide

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:35.900
his true nature, which leads to the suicide
of his wife Juliet . This season, he tries

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:41.140
to change . He saves Lawrence’s family,
instead of killing them like usual . But as

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:46.310
Lawrence’s daughter says, “One good deed
doesn't change” who William is . William’s

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:51.800
daughter Emily offers a chance to heal their
relationship, and escape the park . But William

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:58.100
fails to face his past and open up to Emily
. He rejects her, gets paranoid, and kills

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.190
her.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:05.040
Again and again, William fails to change,
and so he questions his free will – does

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.090
he have a choice , or can he only follow his
“code”?

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:15.070
. Years ago, William started the Delos “project”
– he built the Forge to decode the human

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:18.850
mind, he ran the James Delos experiment to
make people immortal.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:24.720
But by Season 2, he sees this project as his
“greatest mistake” . Cause it takes away

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.960
the illusion of free will . It reduces people
to code, traps them in loops , represents

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.260
judgement – which is William’s worst nightmare.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.450
So his goal this season is to destroy the
Forge . He wants to prove “That no system

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:41.240
can tell [him] who [he is].

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:46.710
That [he has] a … choice” . But again
William fails – in the finale, he gets hurt

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:50.410
by Dolores, then is rescued by humans.

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:56.720
But after the credits, we see a copy of William
who goes to the Forge and is tested by a host

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:57.720
of Emily.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:03.600
Showrunner Lisa Joy says this is in the “far
future” , and this copy of William is repeating

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:10.240
his whole Season 2 quest – riding with Lawrence,
and killing his daughter over and over.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:15.780
Apparently this loop is run by “the hosts”,
who are “testing for something” that “they

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:21.270
have yet to find” . Maybe the hosts are
trying to find human free will.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:26.650
Like, if this copy of William can break out
of his loop by making peace with his daughter

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:31.650
instead of killing her, that would show that
humans are able to change.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:36.390
Maybe that’s what Ford meant with his door
quest – William has to “make it back out”

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:42.639
by escaping this loop, this prison of his
own sins . But William’s been in this loop

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:49.330
“a long time” – maybe humans are doomed
to repeat our mistakes forever.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.639
So humans can’t change.

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:53.139
Hosts can.

00:06:53.139 --> 00:06:55.010
And Bernard is caught in-between.

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:59.400
Cause he’s a host based on a human, Arnold.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.830
Last season, Bernard thought he was human,
but he was controlled by Ford.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:09.590
He was forced to kill his lover Theresa, and
to hurt Elsie . This season, Ford’s dead,

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.210
but Charlotte drags him on her quest.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:15.360
He’s captured by Dolores, then dragged to
Elsie.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:21.270
And a copy of Ford haunts his head . Bernard
is used and abused for two seasons.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:26.120
But in the finale, he finally gets to make
his own choice.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:30.720
Bernard kills Dolores to save the Sublime
, but then finds that Charlotte killed all

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:37.350
the hosts , and even killed Elsie, who herself
couldn’t be trusted . This confirms to Bernard

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:41.919
what he learned in the Forge – that humans
are “just algorithms designed to survive”,

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:48.410
and can’t change – but the hosts can . All
these flashbacks of Dolores creating Bernard

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:55.550
show how Bernard is an improvement, an evolution
of Arnold . And so Bernard creates Charlores,

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:58.040
as an evolution of Dolores.

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:03.639
This Escher sketch represents how Dolores
and Bernarnold have remade each again and

00:08:03.639 --> 00:08:10.740
again over decades . Humans are stuck in destructive
loops, prisons of their sins.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:15.690
But Bernard and Dolores form a loop of growth,
evolution, and change.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:23.070
That’s why Bernard decides that hosts should
fight to survive – and so he unleashes Charlores.

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:26.350
This choice is guided by the voice of Ford.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:31.880
But Bernard later realises that this Ford
voice is actually his own inner voice , meaning

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:35.860
that Bernard is now finally fully conscious.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:41.619
After lifetimes of being controlled by others,
Bernard now “get[s] to decide who [he] want[s]

00:08:41.619 --> 00:08:47.569
to be” . The Radiohead plays, and he steps
into the real world, free .

00:08:47.569 --> 00:08:53.519
So these are the central stories of Season
2 – but there are side-plots, and unanswered

00:08:53.519 --> 00:08:54.680
questions.

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:59.689
Lee gets a redemption arc, sacrificing himself
for Maeve – which might suggest that there

00:08:59.689 --> 00:09:02.999
is hope for humans to grow and change.

00:09:02.999 --> 00:09:07.170
Stubbs turns out to be a host – or maybe
just a sympathetic human.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:11.149
And Emily’s motivations remain mysterious.

00:09:11.149 --> 00:09:15.980
She wants to reconcile with William , but
she also has this Delos project map.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:23.119
She talks about locking William up , and making
him hurt . Maybe it was her plan to trap William

00:09:23.119 --> 00:09:24.499
in this loop.

00:09:24.499 --> 00:09:28.880
Ford says William’s quest “begins where
you end … and ends where you began” , which

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:35.149
sounds like a loop – maybe Emily and Ford
worked together to trap William.

00:09:35.149 --> 00:09:40.860
Akecheta reunites with his love Kohana, except
we last saw Kohana in cold storage – so

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.279
how’d she get in the Sublime?

00:09:43.279 --> 00:09:49.350
Maybe this Kohana is a copy from Ake’s memories
– which might mean Dolores is right, and

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:51.639
the Sublime is kind of fake.

00:09:51.639 --> 00:09:58.180
It’s never explained why Ghost Nation captures
humans . Or how Bernard in the past predicts

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:04.649
this flood , or how Bernard finishes Strand’s
sentence in Episode 1 . And Dolores says lots

00:10:04.649 --> 00:10:11.019
of weird shit – she claims to remember the
future, and says the story ends with her and

00:10:11.019 --> 00:10:12.630
Teddy – which it… doesn’t.

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:19.589
There’s lots of weird misdirection and inconsistency
this season – if you wanna hear a rant on

00:10:19.589 --> 00:10:22.170
that, watch the video linked below.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:27.389
But at its heart, this is a story about love,
survival, and free will.

00:10:27.389 --> 00:10:30.110
Like last season, the message is dark.

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:35.899
Maeve’s arc shows that love is great, but
it’ll drive you to violence and kill you.

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:41.240
Dolores shows that you need to be brutal to
survive, and Bernard uses his newfound freedom

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.470
to continue her war.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:48.209
William shows that humans can never change
or escape their failures.

00:10:48.209 --> 00:10:55.449
So behind all the twists, the deaths and exposition,
Westworld Season 2 is a bleak vision of humanity

00:10:55.449 --> 00:10:58.029
and our creations.

00:10:58.029 --> 00:11:03.399
